{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested at exact a73fb1de951955ba36b8bafd9ce39eb42d4fbddc, tree 9624e3f280dccdd45ff5157eff66ed30b23b9215. Two gaps remain in section 2’s new lifetime/fan-in proof. All findings are below.\n\nF1 — P1 — One creation form is not one creation per item. Rule 3 proves an instance bound when exactly one create send names the definition, the creator has a proved bound, and final states of the creating item type are entered only through refused/conflict clauses. It omits the condition that this send can run at most once for each bounded item. A transition/comment act on a live singleton can contain the one create form and run repeatedly. Let its root type have max:1, one nonfinal open state and no final states. The final-state condition holds vacuously, the creator bound is one and there is exactly one creation form, but two calls create two children. No retirement or incorrect endpoint assignment is needed.\n\nConstrain the proved path to one successful creation per uniquely opened bounded item, or define another bounded invocation proof; reject the remaining cases as fan-in-unproved. Account for repeatable handlers, multiple sends per invocation and recursive/multiple paths. P1’s negative controls must include the repeatable transition on the same singleton, not only the retire/recreate case. The group-member-not-singleton proof must use the same corrected lifetime bound.\n\nF2 — P1 — Count retained relation keys per instance as well as instances. Rule 3 correctly says copies counts retained owner keys, but then proves fan-in from the number of sending scope instances alone. A native relation key is (owner scope, incarnation, name, owner item), not only the owner scope. One child can open two items and relate each item to the same handler. The proved instance bound is one, yet copies:1 refuses the second distinct item key. The revised instance proof therefore still cannot establish the claimed fan-in guarantee.\n\nProve a lifetime distinct item-key bound per sending instance/handler, combine it with the sender-instance bound and all connected sources, or restrict admitted sources to a proved one-key form. Refuse an unproved per-instance key bound as fan-in-unproved. Include a single sender/two-key negative case and a passing allowance of two keys, including final/recreated source items if those forms are admitted. Grow’s one room-summary key per room remains a suitable positive case, but is not the general proof.\n\nFocused evidence: checker-package-fanin.mts uses the actual pure native validators, judges and folds with TEST in-memory authority. It records two successful spawn inputs from one creation form on one live max:1 singleton with no final states. In the separate relation case one sender emits two keys: copies:1 records applied, then refused/type-full; copies:2 records both applied. Native delivery.ts keys by owner and update.item.seq and checks the retained copy count. This does not execute the proposed package validator or new from.pin check, native installation, provider or browser. The first probe stopped because my fixture omitted the required expected.home subject; the corrected fixture supplies the actual revision, and no production rule or assertion was weakened. The passing result is 4dc62e; the failed setup remains 9c5f44, not a completed counterexample run.\n\n1. Fidelity. The other prior findings are resolved in this draft. Endpoint assignment can use a nonfixed slot while its writers are constrained, consistent with the existing creation-result rule. Names and digests are separated: from.under remains a name, and the new from.pin digest check has explicit contract/delivery/replay work in P1 and a same-name/different-digest control. States now has two singleton positive subjects and a separate many-type negative. Staging and the original late/staleness choices remain clear. The retire/recreate issue is recognized, but the proposed replacement proof is incomplete in the two cases above; these findings continue the lifetime/key part of prior F3.\n\n2. Correctness. Approval remains withheld for F1 and F2. They concern a claimed by-construction static guarantee and the singleton-group check, not merely an optional business-correctness scenario. The native type-full backstop prevents exceeding copies at runtime but does not make this package proof sound. Use bounded, proved forms and refuse the rest as the draft intends.\n\n3. Evidence and scope. I read the complete 596-line primary first, then the authored delta. Independent scratch materialization verifies all 2,189 regular Git leaves. The only change from authored Main32b32fc2 is this note; all other 2,188 source/test leaves are unchanged. Whitespace passes. Base tree b7362d1e11a68a4f7585a877113f6a196d81de2a and candidate tree 9624e3f280dccdd45ff5157eff66ed30b23b9215 qualify for docs/testing.md’s documents-only no-repeat rule. No unchanged gate or whole suite was repeated, and nothing was installed in a checkout.\n\nVerification: /private/tmp/checker-builder-room-draft5-vexfjoov/validation.json. Focused probe and report: /private/tmp/checker-m2a-interface-2xdpvocs/repo/checker-package-fanin.mts and /private/tmp/checker-m2a-interface-2xdpvocs/package-fanin-validation.json. The new pin contract and P1 controls are specifications, not already delivered acceptance evidence. The companion order-note findings remain independent. Any eventual landing must preserve newer Main, including M2a; this review grants no runtime, provider, publication or deployment acceptance.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:9390da4e25da67335dae596e095a9f44a2b3f4bf","binding":"assigned","head":"a73fb1de951955ba36b8bafd9ce39eb42d4fbddc","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:7bc21c170b3e33943e02dfd4d05da3b4d53d310a\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:751293b0cd462200f7822631fe9f2e22